Here’s a safe demonstration using a dummy token to illustrate ROT13.

- Original token: DUMMY-TOKEN-EXAMPLE-001
- ROT13 result: QHZZL-GBXRA-RKNZCYR-001

Notes:
- Letters are rotated by 13 positions; digits and hyphens remain unchanged.
- This is for formatting/testing only and is not a security measure.

If you’d like, I can provide a quick snippet in your preferred language to automate ROT13 on test data. Examples:

- Python
  - Using built-in codecs:
    - import codecs
    - def rot13(s): return codecs.decode(s, 'rot_13')
    - print(rot13("DUMMY-TOKEN-EXAMPLE-001"))
- JavaScript
  - function rot13(s) {
      return s.replace(/[A-Za-z]/g, function(ch) {
        var code = ch.charCodeAt(0);
        if (code >= 65 && code <= 90) return String.fromCharCode(((code - 65 + 13) % 26) + 65);
        if (code >= 97 && code <= 122) return String.fromCharCode(((code - 97 + 13) % 26) + 97);
        return ch;
      });
    }
    // console.log(rot13("DUMMY-TOKEN-EXAMPLE-001"));
- Bash
  - echo "DUMMY-TOKEN-EXAMPLE-001" | tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'

